<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rohilla State</h1><p class="st-meta">1721 CE – 1774 CE (approx.) · capital Aonla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Afghan state in the plains of Rohilkhand, north-east of Delhi, built by Rohilla soldiers in the decline of the Mughal Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Afghan soldiers, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>, settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katehr">Katehr</a> region east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> from the late 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohammed-khan">Ali Muhammad Khan</a>, adopted son of a Rohilla chief, built a power base there from the 1720s, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aonla">Aonla</a> his capital, and was recognised by the Mughal court as nawab.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohammed-khan">Ali Muhammad</a>'s death in 1748, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a> ruled as regent for his sons and led a confederacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohilla</a> chiefs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bareilly">Bareilly</a>. The Rohillas supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761. They promoted Afghan settlement, built towns and mosques, and patronised Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> learning.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1774 the Nawab of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/awadh-state">Awadh State</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> troops, defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilla-war">Rohilla War</a>. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohilla</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan">Faizullah Khan</a> kept the small state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rampur-state">Rampur</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1721 CE – 1774 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.95%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1721 CE – 1774 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aonla</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Muhammad Khan, Hafiz Rahmat Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian, Hindustani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rohilkhand, Rohilla Afghans of Bareilly</dd></dl></aside></div>

